## The problem

Each enterprise client wanted the same five capabilities — lead management, KPI
tracking, project management, attendance, and payroll — with different fields,
different approval rules, and different definitions of what a "closed" lead
means. The obvious options were both bad.

Deploying a separate instance per client means every bug fix becomes an N-way
deployment and every client drifts onto their own fork within a year. One shared
instance with a `tenant_id` column everywhere means the entire security model
rests on every developer, forever, remembering to add one `WHERE` clause.

The second failure mode is the one that actually happens. It only takes a single
missed filter in a single query, once, and one client sees another client's
pipeline. For a CRM holding commercial data, that is not a bug — it is the end
of the contract.

## Architecture

Shared schema, shared deployment, isolation enforced below the application.

The principle I designed around: **a developer writing a new query should not be
able to leak data even if they forget the tenant filter entirely.** If safety
depends on discipline, it will eventually fail.

Every request resolves a tenant context at the edge, before any handler runs.
That context is bound to the request, not to a global, and the data layer refuses
to issue a query without one.

```ts
// The data layer takes tenant scope as a required argument. There is no
// unscoped export — a query that forgets it does not compile.
export function tenantClient(tenantId: string) {
  return prisma.$extends({
    query: {
      $allModels: {
        async $allOperations({ args, query, model }) {
          if (TENANT_SCOPED.has(model)) {
            args.where = { ...args.where, tenantId };
          }
          return query(args);
        },
      },
    },
  });
}

// Handlers never touch `prisma` directly.
export async function listLeads(ctx: RequestContext, filter: LeadFilter) {
  const db = tenantClient(ctx.tenantId);
  return db.lead.findMany({ where: toPrismaWhere(filter) });
}
```

Two things make this hold. The extension injects the tenant predicate into every
operation on a scoped model, so forgetting it in a handler is not possible. And
the unscoped client is not exported from the module at all — reaching around the
boundary requires deliberately editing the data layer, which is exactly the kind
of change that gets noticed in review.

**RBAC on top of tenancy.** These are separate concerns and conflating them was
a mistake I made early. Tenancy answers *which rows exist for you*. Roles answer
*what you may do with the rows you can see*. Once separated, per-client role
configuration became data rather than code — a client can define a "Regional
Manager" without a deployment.

**Queues for anything slow.** Payroll runs, report generation, and bulk imports
were making requests hang for tens of seconds. Moving them onto BullMQ with Redis
took them off the request path entirely; the API returns a job id immediately and
the client polls or receives a socket update. Perceived responsiveness improved
by roughly 40%.

## Challenges

**Migrations across live tenants.** One schema, several clients, no shared
maintenance window. Every migration has to be backwards-compatible with the code
currently running — add columns before writing them, write both before reading
the new one, drop the old only once nothing reads it. Slower per change, but it
means no client ever needs to be told about a deploy.

**Configurability without becoming a form builder.** Every client wants custom
fields, and it is tempting to build a generic schema engine. That path ends in a
product where nothing has a type and every query is dynamic. I constrained
customisation to typed field definitions per module with a fixed set of field
types. Some requests get declined. The system stays queryable.

**Noisy-neighbour load.** A shared deployment means one client's bulk import can
degrade everyone. Per-tenant queue concurrency limits fixed the worst of it —
a large job now takes longer for that tenant rather than for all of them.

## Outcome

Five modules running in production across multiple enterprise accounts on a
single deployment, with tenant isolation enforced at the data layer and
configurable RBAC per client.

The lesson I carry forward is that isolation belongs as low in the stack as you
can push it. Every layer above the enforcement point is a layer where a
reasonable person can make a reasonable mistake. Moving the boundary down does
not make developers better — it makes their mistakes non-fatal.

I have written up the Postgres-specific version of this argument in
[Row-Level Security is the tenant isolation you actually want](/engineering/postgres-row-level-security-multi-tenant).
